1
00:00:01,835 --> 00:00:02,669
Carbon.

2
00:00:05,755 --> 00:00:06,715
Hydrogen.

3
00:00:09,300 --> 00:00:10,135
Carbon.

4
00:00:13,221 --> 00:00:14,556
Carbon.

5
00:00:15,682 --> 00:00:18,143
And last but not least, carbon.

6
00:00:19,978 --> 00:00:21,813
This might be the glue talking,

7
00:00:21,896 --> 00:00:25,108
but that was a very pleasurable
139 and a half hours.

8
00:00:25,233 --> 00:00:26,151
Agreed.

9
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:28,361
What kind of DNA is this, anyway?

10
00:00:28,445 --> 00:00:32,574
I was trying to design the genetic code
of an advanced race of human being.

11
00:00:32,657 --> 00:00:34,159
One of my rare failures, actually.

12
00:00:34,242 --> 00:00:37,704
-What went wrong?
-The balls kept sticking to my pants.

13
00:00:39,205 --> 00:00:41,458
Let's just let it dry.

14
00:00:41,541 --> 00:00:42,959
-Hi.
-Hello.

15
00:00:43,042 --> 00:00:45,253
-Oh, don't slam the--!
-Don't slam the--! [gasps]

16
00:00:48,548 --> 00:00:50,258
That was exhilarating.

17
00:00:52,093 --> 00:00:53,428
-Pick up your dresses?
-[Amy] Yep.

18
00:00:53,511 --> 00:00:57,974
The tailor had to take mine in
and let Penny's out. Best day of my life.

19
00:00:59,893 --> 00:01:02,020
What about the day you met me?

20
00:01:02,103 --> 00:01:03,438
I stand by my statement.

21
00:01:04,439 --> 00:01:07,984
Well, thank you guys so much for the clothes,
and the shoes, the plane tickets.

22
00:01:08,068 --> 00:01:09,110
You've been so generous.

23
00:01:09,194 --> 00:01:12,238
It's important that all our friends
get to share this moment with us.

24
00:01:12,322 --> 00:01:16,159
And then for years to come, you can tell others
you had a front-row seat to history.

25
00:01:16,242 --> 00:01:19,746
Although, technically, I think your seats
are in the second row.

26
00:01:19,829 --> 00:01:22,457
You wanna go back to the apartment
and see me try on my dress?

27
00:01:22,624 --> 00:01:25,418
Shouldn't I see it for the first time
at the Nobel ceremony?

28
00:01:25,502 --> 00:01:26,336
It's not a wedding.

29
00:01:26,419 --> 00:01:29,756
Okay, well, give me a minute to think
of another reason I don't wanna see it.

30
00:01:30,924 --> 00:01:33,927
You can practice your acceptance speech
while I try on the dress.

31
00:01:34,010 --> 00:01:36,888
Ah, great. You can help me whittle it down
to 90 minutes.

32
00:01:42,936 --> 00:01:45,522
Was Amy suspicious
when they had to let your dress out?

33
00:01:45,605 --> 00:01:48,608
No, she was so happy
she didn't even question it.

34
00:01:48,691 --> 00:01:50,944
Someone will figure it out.
Why don't we tell people?

35
00:01:51,027 --> 00:01:53,363
No, it's too early.
I haven't wrapped my head around it.

36
00:01:53,488 --> 00:01:54,697
I have. My head is wrapped.

37
00:01:54,781 --> 00:01:55,949
Yeah, well...

38
00:01:56,032 --> 00:01:58,868
If something else had been wrapped,
we wouldn't be in this situation.

39
00:02:00,119 --> 00:02:03,832
Excuse me, but if I recall, you were the one
who went out drinking with Sheldon,

40
00:02:03,915 --> 00:02:07,836
-then came home and attacked me.
-Attacked you? I think I said, "Do you wanna?"

41
00:02:07,919 --> 00:02:09,712
Well, yeah.

42
00:02:09,796 --> 00:02:11,881
-I was helpless.
-Uh-huh.

43
00:02:13,132 --> 00:02:15,593
So do you wanna?

44
00:02:17,345 --> 00:02:19,264
-Well, I can't get more pregnant.
-Hmm.

45
00:02:19,347 --> 00:02:21,057
-We'll see about that.
-Mm-hm.

46
00:02:21,766 --> 00:02:25,061
♪ Our whole universe was in
A hot, dense state ♪

47
00:02:25,144 --> 00:02:29,023
♪ Then nearly 14 billion years ago
Expansion started-- Wait ♪

48
00:02:29,107 --> 00:02:31,776
♪ The Earth began to cool
The autotrophs began to drool ♪

49
00:02:31,860 --> 00:02:34,070
♪ Neanderthals developed tools
We built a wall ♪

50
00:02:34,154 --> 00:02:35,280
♪ We built the pyramids ♪

51
00:02:35,363 --> 00:02:37,991
♪ Math, science, history
Unraveling the mystery ♪

52
00:02:38,074 --> 00:02:40,201
♪ It all started with a big bang ♪

53
00:02:40,285 --> 00:02:41,494
♪ Bang! ♪

54
00:02:45,665 --> 00:02:48,960
Now, Michael, Mommy and Daddy
are going away for a couple days,

55
00:02:49,085 --> 00:02:51,796
so if you're gonna say your first words,
you've gotta to do it now

56
00:02:51,880 --> 00:02:53,214
or wait until we get back.

57
00:02:56,801 --> 00:02:59,262
Somebody just peed in the big-girl potty.

58
00:02:59,345 --> 00:03:01,222
[Bernadette] Oh, I'm so proud.

59
00:03:01,347 --> 00:03:02,599
It was her, right?

60
00:03:03,725 --> 00:03:05,310
Yes.

61
00:03:05,393 --> 00:03:09,439
You still feel good about leaving them
with Stuart and Denise for a couple days?

62
00:03:09,522 --> 00:03:10,648
I guess.

63
00:03:10,732 --> 00:03:12,817
I just hope we don't miss Michael's first words.

64
00:03:12,901 --> 00:03:14,903
Or Stuart's last words.

65
00:03:16,237 --> 00:03:19,365
You realize it's been years
since we've got away just the two of us.

66
00:03:19,449 --> 00:03:21,659
I know. I can't wait.

67
00:03:21,743 --> 00:03:24,662
A fancy hotel room with a big bed.

68
00:03:24,746 --> 00:03:28,333
Yeah, we are gonna sleep our asses off.

69
00:03:32,045 --> 00:03:34,964
You really think walking her
is gonna help me meet girls?

70
00:03:35,048 --> 00:03:36,591
Cinnamon is chick bait.

71
00:03:37,634 --> 00:03:39,177
Good. Because I'm not.

72
00:03:40,470 --> 00:03:43,973
Just remember, you're in charge.
Don't let her pull you.

73
00:03:46,100 --> 00:03:49,687
That seems
like an unnecessary thing to say.

74
00:03:49,771 --> 00:03:54,150
And please, if you have any problems,
any questions, call me immediately, okay?

75
00:03:54,233 --> 00:03:56,152
She's my baby.

76
00:03:56,236 --> 00:03:58,363
It shouldn't come up, but just in case,

77
00:03:58,446 --> 00:04:01,324
where could I buy a dog
that looks exactly like her?

78
00:04:03,534 --> 00:04:05,662
Kidding. If she dies I'll just tell you.

79
00:04:08,748 --> 00:04:10,833
-Ready?
-Hang on.

80
00:04:14,587 --> 00:04:15,755
Go.

81
00:04:15,838 --> 00:04:17,715
Your Majesties,
members of the Nobel Academy,

82
00:04:17,799 --> 00:04:20,468
when I was a young boy,
I always knew I'd wind up on this stage.

83
00:04:20,551 --> 00:04:22,553
Everybody who said I wouldn't
looks foolish now.

84
00:04:22,637 --> 00:04:24,472
I'm talking about you, Mr. Hubert Givens--

85
00:04:24,555 --> 00:04:27,642
Sheldon.
Sheldon, why are you talking so fast?

86
00:04:27,725 --> 00:04:29,978
I'm trying to get my speech
down to 90 minutes.

87
00:04:31,020 --> 00:04:33,356
Nobody's gonna understand a word
you're saying.

88
00:04:33,439 --> 00:04:34,857
Welcome to my life.

89
00:04:36,651 --> 00:04:38,194
I think you need to do some editing.

90
00:04:38,277 --> 00:04:40,822
Like maybe cut out all that stuff
about your childhood.

91
00:04:40,905 --> 00:04:43,992
Are you kidding?
Growing up in the backwoods of East Texas

92
00:04:44,075 --> 00:04:45,702
is what makes me warm and relatable.

93
00:04:47,412 --> 00:04:49,831
What about all these pages
calling out everyone

94
00:04:49,914 --> 00:04:51,457
who said you wouldn't succeed?

95
00:04:51,541 --> 00:04:53,501
I told them all they would rue the day.

96
00:04:53,584 --> 00:04:55,920
How is it gonna make me look
if the day finally comes

97
00:04:56,004 --> 00:04:57,588
and they're not filled with rue?

98
00:04:58,673 --> 00:05:01,175
Sheldon, this isn't about rueing.

99
00:05:01,259 --> 00:05:04,887
This is about humbly accepting
a great honor.

100
00:05:04,971 --> 00:05:07,849
Amy, we won the Nobel Prize in physics.

101
00:05:07,932 --> 00:05:10,393
Humility is for people
who win the goofy Nobels,

102
00:05:10,476 --> 00:05:12,770
like literature, economics and peace.

103
00:05:14,147 --> 00:05:17,108
-Please tell me that's not in your speech.
-[scoffs]

104
00:05:17,191 --> 00:05:19,318
I can cut it, but it's the only joke I have.

105
00:05:23,239 --> 00:05:24,240
All right, bagels down.

106
00:05:24,324 --> 00:05:26,784
Before we head to the airport,
I'd like to go over a few things.

107
00:05:26,868 --> 00:05:29,203
[all groan]

108
00:05:29,287 --> 00:05:31,539
From the moment we step off the plane,

109
00:05:31,622 --> 00:05:34,834
each and every one of you
is an ambassador for Amy and myself.

110
00:05:34,917 --> 00:05:38,212
I told you these tickets weren't free.

111
00:05:38,296 --> 00:05:41,674
As such, your behavior must reflect
the highest standards.

112
00:05:41,758 --> 00:05:43,342
Raj, bagel down.

113
00:05:44,635 --> 00:05:47,138
If my blood sugar drops and I get bitchy,
it's on you.

114
00:05:48,598 --> 00:05:51,517
-Amy, why don't you kick us off?
-Thank you.

115
00:05:51,601 --> 00:05:55,772
"The challenging climate of Scandinavia
has left its mark on the Swedish character.

116
00:05:57,148 --> 00:06:01,277
These dour latter-day Vikings
are slow to warm up to strangers,

117
00:06:01,360 --> 00:06:06,574
but if you follow a few easy steps,
a Swede can be your friend for life."

118
00:06:06,657 --> 00:06:10,912
All right. Number one. "In Sweden,
punctuality is taken very seriously.

119
00:06:10,995 --> 00:06:15,416
In other words, the loosey-goosey attitude
in Helsinki will not fly in Stockholm."

120
00:06:16,042 --> 00:06:19,962
Two. "At the beginning and end
of all business and social meetings,

121
00:06:20,046 --> 00:06:21,589
shake hands with everyone present.

122
00:06:21,672 --> 00:06:23,257
Men, women and children."

123
00:06:23,341 --> 00:06:25,676
Yes, you're all encouraged to pair off
and practice this

124
00:06:25,760 --> 00:06:28,513
once we're in the air
and the seat-belt sign is off.

125
00:06:28,596 --> 00:06:29,764
-[clears throat]
-Yes, Penny.

126
00:06:29,847 --> 00:06:31,516
Oh, do we have to go?

127
00:06:33,476 --> 00:06:36,938
Yes. And a reminder, Penny,
there's free alcohol in business class.

128
00:06:37,021 --> 00:06:39,023
-Don't abuse it.
-Relax, I'm not gonna drink.

129
00:06:39,107 --> 00:06:40,233
Why? You pregnant?

130
00:06:40,316 --> 00:06:42,485
What--? [laughs] No.

131
00:06:42,568 --> 00:06:45,530
No. I just, you know--
I don't like to drink when I fly.

132
00:06:45,655 --> 00:06:48,741
Oh, please,
I've seen you drink in the shower.

133
00:06:51,452 --> 00:06:53,579
You guys have showered together?

134
00:06:57,375 --> 00:06:59,502
Sometimes you're just...

135
00:06:59,585 --> 00:07:00,503
ick.

136
00:07:02,421 --> 00:07:03,798
That wasn't a no.

137
00:07:05,550 --> 00:07:09,053
We should head out now if we're gonna get
to the airport six hours before boarding.

138
00:07:10,221 --> 00:07:11,305
Excuse me. Hello?

139
00:07:11,389 --> 00:07:14,308
What did we just learn
about the end of business meetings?

140
00:07:14,392 --> 00:07:16,519
Oh, my God. Ugh.

141
00:07:18,229 --> 00:07:19,647
-Yeah.
-Yeah, great.

142
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:24,819
You first, go.

143
00:07:27,405 --> 00:07:28,281
[Amy] Wait.

144
00:07:28,364 --> 00:07:30,199
-[Penny] Here, you just--
-[Bernadette] Help me!

145
00:07:30,283 --> 00:07:31,826
-Help me!
-[Penny] Wait. Come here.

146
00:07:31,909 --> 00:07:33,786
-[Penny] Yeah. Get in.
-We got this.

147
00:07:33,870 --> 00:07:36,122
[all yell]

148
00:07:36,205 --> 00:07:38,583
Hang on, guys. I've got an idea.

149
00:07:44,964 --> 00:07:46,090
Well done.

150
00:07:46,174 --> 00:07:50,469
You know, you're proving to be
an invaluable part of my entourage.

151
00:07:50,553 --> 00:07:51,762
Cool.

152
00:08:03,983 --> 00:08:05,568
-Yeah?
-How you doing?

153
00:08:05,651 --> 00:08:06,694
Fine, why?

154
00:08:06,777 --> 00:08:09,947
Well, this is the furthest
we've ever been away from the kids.

155
00:08:10,031 --> 00:08:12,533
I was just checking to see if you're okay.

156
00:08:12,617 --> 00:08:15,119
I'm on my second Jack Reacher.
I'm doing great.

157
00:08:16,204 --> 00:08:18,414
Good, me too.
I was just looking at the map.

158
00:08:18,498 --> 00:08:21,501
Couldn't help thinking we're here
and they're there,

159
00:08:21,584 --> 00:08:24,212
and if anything happened,
we'd have to go here

160
00:08:24,295 --> 00:08:26,339
to get all the way back there.

161
00:08:26,464 --> 00:08:28,382
Why are you trying to freak me out?

162
00:08:28,466 --> 00:08:32,011
This is our vacation.
I thought we should do things together.

163
00:08:33,512 --> 00:08:34,722
Howie, the kids are okay.

164
00:08:34,805 --> 00:08:38,726
-How do you know that?
-I just know. A mother knows.

165
00:08:38,809 --> 00:08:41,520
So, what, now the Force is with you?

166
00:08:41,604 --> 00:08:43,272
Let's see.

167
00:08:43,356 --> 00:08:46,067
This is not the woman you want to annoy.

168
00:08:48,361 --> 00:08:49,445
Howard. Howard.

169
00:08:49,529 --> 00:08:52,281
-What?
-You see the woman sitting next to me?

170
00:08:53,407 --> 00:08:54,992
[Howard] What about her?

171
00:08:55,076 --> 00:08:56,410
Is this her?

172
00:08:58,162 --> 00:09:01,457
-Buffy the Vampire Slayer?
-Shh! Be cool.

173
00:09:03,751 --> 00:09:04,961
No, that's not her.

174
00:09:05,044 --> 00:09:07,171
Okay. How about now?

175
00:09:11,676 --> 00:09:13,052
Uh-oh.

176
00:09:19,183 --> 00:09:22,228
That's the fourth time she's been
to the bathroom in the last two hours.

177
00:09:23,437 --> 00:09:24,689
So?

178
00:09:24,772 --> 00:09:27,483
Her breakfast was binding.
I made sure of that.

179
00:09:28,985 --> 00:09:29,902
So?

180
00:09:29,986 --> 00:09:32,280
So she clearly has some sort
of stomach distress.

181
00:09:32,363 --> 00:09:34,907
If it's viral, we're all susceptible.

182
00:09:34,991 --> 00:09:37,827
-She's probably just airsick.
-What if she's not?

183
00:09:37,952 --> 00:09:41,289
What if we get what she has?
What if we infect the king of Sweden?

184
00:09:41,372 --> 00:09:42,999
That's how wars start.

185
00:09:49,213 --> 00:09:51,841
All right, that's it.
This is Outbreak and she's the monkey.

186
00:09:53,551 --> 00:09:55,886
[quietly] Wait, stop, be reasonable.

187
00:09:57,972 --> 00:10:01,100
Leonard.
We need to do something about your wife.

188
00:10:01,183 --> 00:10:02,018
What's the matter?

189
00:10:02,101 --> 00:10:04,270
She's sick and will
take us all down with her.

190
00:10:04,353 --> 00:10:06,022
She's not sick, Sheldon.

191
00:10:06,105 --> 00:10:09,567
She is, and I'm gonna catch it,
and it's gonna ruin the greatest day of my life.

192
00:10:09,650 --> 00:10:12,486
I promise,
you're not gonna get what she has.

193
00:10:12,570 --> 00:10:15,656
-What's going on?
-[gasps] Unclean! Unclean!

194
00:10:15,740 --> 00:10:18,117
-What?
-He thinks you're sick.

195
00:10:18,200 --> 00:10:20,369
Oh.

196
00:10:20,453 --> 00:10:21,912
Should we tell him?

197
00:10:21,996 --> 00:10:24,206
If we don't,
he might try and jump out of the plane.

198
00:10:24,290 --> 00:10:26,709
Eh. That doesn't answer my question.

199
00:10:26,792 --> 00:10:27,835
[Sheldon] Tell me what?

200
00:10:29,587 --> 00:10:30,921
I'm pregnant, Sheldon.

201
00:10:32,298 --> 00:10:34,258
You're right. I can't catch that.

202
00:10:38,763 --> 00:10:41,682
Good news, Amy. She's just pregnant.

203
00:10:45,770 --> 00:10:46,979
Are you kidding me?

204
00:10:47,063 --> 00:10:51,901
You just found out a woman who has loved
and cared for you for 12 years is pregnant

205
00:10:51,984 --> 00:10:55,946
and all you can say is you're relieved
she's not gonna get you sick?

206
00:10:56,030 --> 00:10:58,616
There's no need for a recap. I was there.

207
00:11:00,868 --> 00:11:03,037
-Why didn't you tell me?
-I didn't tell anybody.

208
00:11:03,120 --> 00:11:04,955
I'm not anybody. I'm your best friend.

209
00:11:05,039 --> 00:11:07,166
-[Bernadette] What's going on?
-She's pregnant.

210
00:11:07,249 --> 00:11:09,126
That's fantastic!

211
00:11:09,210 --> 00:11:10,711
Why didn't you tell me?

212
00:11:11,796 --> 00:11:14,590
-She didn't tell me either.
-But I'm her best friend.

213
00:11:14,673 --> 00:11:17,134
[scoffs] We'll get into that later.

214
00:11:18,427 --> 00:11:20,471
What happened
to "I'm never gonna have kids"?

215
00:11:20,554 --> 00:11:23,933
It was an accident.
I went out drinking with Sheldon.

216
00:11:24,016 --> 00:11:25,267
Oh, my gosh.

217
00:11:25,351 --> 00:11:29,855
-We're sister wives?
-No. Then I went home and slept with Leonard.

218
00:11:30,022 --> 00:11:32,817
-While fantasizing about--?
-Leonard.

219
00:11:32,900 --> 00:11:35,986
-And a little Idris Elba. I mean, ahh.
-Oh, yeah.

220
00:11:37,446 --> 00:11:41,200
-What would you like me to say?
-How about congratulating us?

221
00:11:41,283 --> 00:11:44,495
Are congratulations even in order?
I didn't think Penny wanted children.

222
00:11:44,662 --> 00:11:45,830
She didn't, now she does.

223
00:11:45,913 --> 00:11:48,040
And just so you know,
we weren't gonna tell anybody

224
00:11:48,124 --> 00:11:49,750
so we wouldn't upstage your big day.

225
00:11:49,834 --> 00:11:53,587
Oh, please, you couldn't upstage us.
We won a Nobel Prize.

226
00:11:53,671 --> 00:11:57,091
-Any idiot can have a baby.
-Hey, what's going on?

227
00:11:57,174 --> 00:11:58,092
Case in point.

228
00:12:00,261 --> 00:12:02,263
You are a selfish jerk.

229
00:12:02,346 --> 00:12:04,598
To hell with you and your Nobel Prize.

230
00:12:06,809 --> 00:12:10,146
I found her boarding pass in her purse.
It's totally her.

231
00:12:13,983 --> 00:12:16,694
Hey, Stuart, just checking in.
Seeing if everything's okay.

232
00:12:16,777 --> 00:12:19,113
Oh, yeah, we're having fun.

233
00:12:20,114 --> 00:12:22,950
Me, Halley and Denise
played hide-and-seek all day.

234
00:12:23,033 --> 00:12:24,410
Oh, that's nice.

235
00:12:24,493 --> 00:12:27,663
Yeah. I found Denise right away.

236
00:12:29,457 --> 00:12:30,875
Where was Halley hiding?

237
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:32,209
Uh...

238
00:12:32,293 --> 00:12:34,545
the important thing is she's not there now.

239
00:12:35,629 --> 00:12:39,091
-Okay, so everyone's happy and healthy?
-Well, that depends...

240
00:12:39,175 --> 00:12:40,259
What's that mean?

241
00:12:40,342 --> 00:12:44,013
Uh, how many teeth did Halley have
when you left?

242
00:12:45,431 --> 00:12:46,307
All of them!

243
00:12:46,390 --> 00:12:47,475
Oh, yeah.

244
00:12:47,558 --> 00:12:49,643
That's what I was afraid of.

245
00:12:51,061 --> 00:12:51,896
What happened?

246
00:12:51,979 --> 00:12:56,650
Um, well, all right, um,
Michael had a little fever last night--

247
00:12:56,734 --> 00:12:58,110
Michael had a fever?

248
00:12:58,194 --> 00:13:01,530
-Wanna hear about Halley or not?
-What happened, Stuart?

249
00:13:01,614 --> 00:13:04,742
He was running a little fever,
nothing to worry about.

250
00:13:04,825 --> 00:13:06,952
And Halley, bless her heart,

251
00:13:07,036 --> 00:13:11,290
wanted to bring him his boo-boo bear,
so she climbed over the safety rail

252
00:13:11,373 --> 00:13:14,084
and took a little tumble down the stairs.

253
00:13:15,419 --> 00:13:16,754
She fell down the stairs?

254
00:13:16,837 --> 00:13:21,967
[stammers] She rolled down the stairs,
laughing the whole time.

255
00:13:22,051 --> 00:13:25,596
Anyway, when she got to the bottom,
there was a tooth missing.

256
00:13:25,679 --> 00:13:26,847
Oh, Stuart--

257
00:13:26,931 --> 00:13:29,308
She's fine. She thought it was funny.

258
00:13:30,851 --> 00:13:34,855
-Did you save it for the Tooth Fairy?
-No, we couldn't find it.

259
00:13:34,939 --> 00:13:40,486
We have a theory about where it is,
but it'll take six to eight hours to confirm.

260
00:13:43,113 --> 00:13:46,825
Speaking of which,
where do you keep the spaghetti strainer?

261
00:13:50,454 --> 00:13:53,374
You didn't seriously expect him to react
like a normal human being.

262
00:13:53,457 --> 00:13:58,587
No, but still, after all these years,
after all the crap I've put up with,

263
00:13:58,671 --> 00:14:01,966
you'd think just this once
he'd care about someone else's feelings.

264
00:14:02,091 --> 00:14:03,217
Oh, my God!

265
00:14:03,300 --> 00:14:06,303
-What?
-Pickled herring. Who knew how good it was!

266
00:14:07,388 --> 00:14:10,766
-Really?
-Sounds gross, looks gross, smells gross...

267
00:14:10,849 --> 00:14:12,560
It's delicious!

268
00:14:13,894 --> 00:14:14,895
[knocking on door]

269
00:14:14,979 --> 00:14:16,855
Ooh, that might be my salted cod!

270
00:14:20,192 --> 00:14:23,195
-What?
-Sheldon has something he'd like to say.

271
00:14:23,279 --> 00:14:25,614
I'm sorry I didn't react appropriately.

272
00:14:25,698 --> 00:14:27,825
You and Penny are bringing new life
into the world.

273
00:14:27,908 --> 00:14:29,868
Congratulations. I can't wait to meet it.

274
00:14:30,035 --> 00:14:32,538
-It?
-That's a gender-neutral pronoun.

275
00:14:32,621 --> 00:14:34,957
If you're offended,
take it up with the English language.

276
00:14:36,500 --> 00:14:38,460
Oh, good, you're here. Listen.

277
00:14:38,544 --> 00:14:41,797
We're thinking
maybe we should go back to L.A.

278
00:14:41,922 --> 00:14:44,383
-Why?
-We just can't be this far away from the kids.

279
00:14:44,466 --> 00:14:48,304
Bernie's having a meltdown,
and frankly, so am I.

280
00:14:48,387 --> 00:14:51,223
Well, pull it together.
This is a big day for me.

281
00:14:53,559 --> 00:14:56,604
Yeah, I wasn't sure what to do.

282
00:14:56,687 --> 00:14:58,355
Now I am. We're going home.

283
00:14:58,439 --> 00:15:00,649
-We'll join you.
-Do I get a vote in this?

284
00:15:00,733 --> 00:15:03,360
-They'll have pickled herring on the plane.
-Bye-bye.

285
00:15:06,655 --> 00:15:11,660
Oh, I hate to say it,
but I think everyone is being incredibly selfish.

286
00:15:11,744 --> 00:15:15,289
Well, you would be the authority
on the subject.

287
00:15:16,749 --> 00:15:18,792
What does that mean?

288
00:15:18,876 --> 00:15:23,547
Sheldon, no one is happier than I am
to win the Nobel,

289
00:15:23,631 --> 00:15:26,300
but it's not more important than our friends.

290
00:15:27,676 --> 00:15:31,138
How can you call them friends
when they're abandoning us?

291
00:15:31,221 --> 00:15:34,350
They're abandoning us
because you broke their hearts.

292
00:15:37,978 --> 00:15:40,814
-I didn't mean to.
-I know.

293
00:15:40,898 --> 00:15:42,608
You never mean to.

294
00:15:43,984 --> 00:15:46,654
That's the only reason people tolerate you.

295
00:15:48,906 --> 00:15:50,324
Does that include you?

296
00:15:53,077 --> 00:15:54,828
Sometimes, yeah.

297
00:16:00,250 --> 00:16:01,669
[Penny vomits]

298
00:16:02,962 --> 00:16:03,879
[toilet flushes]

299
00:16:03,963 --> 00:16:05,214
[Penny groans]

300
00:16:05,297 --> 00:16:07,591
Okay. That's it for the fish.

301
00:16:07,675 --> 00:16:12,304
We'll be back with the meatballs
after a short word from our sponsor.

302
00:16:12,388 --> 00:16:13,597
Oh. Thank you.

303
00:16:16,809 --> 00:16:19,144
Leonard, I can't go home.

304
00:16:19,228 --> 00:16:20,854
I have to be there for Amy.

305
00:16:20,938 --> 00:16:23,649
Yeah, I was thinking that too.

306
00:16:23,732 --> 00:16:27,319
As angry as I am at Sheldon,
I still wanna see him win that medal.

307
00:16:27,528 --> 00:16:33,158
It's so strange,
no matter how thoughtless and selfish he is,

308
00:16:33,242 --> 00:16:34,827
I still love him.

309
00:16:34,910 --> 00:16:38,706
If you think about it,
he has kind of been our practice kid.

310
00:16:40,165 --> 00:16:44,294
Like when you make pancakes
and the first one comes out a little wonky.

311
00:16:45,587 --> 00:16:47,548
The university prefers "quirky."

312
00:16:48,757 --> 00:16:50,259
[phone rings]

313
00:16:52,594 --> 00:16:54,805
Hello. Hey. It's Howard.

314
00:16:56,390 --> 00:16:57,599
Oh, us too.

315
00:16:57,683 --> 00:16:58,851
Wait, what?

316
00:16:58,934 --> 00:17:02,896
They're gonna stay.
The kids are fine. Bernie's parents took over.

317
00:17:02,980 --> 00:17:03,981
Really?

318
00:17:04,064 --> 00:17:06,066
Oh, poor little guy.

319
00:17:06,150 --> 00:17:07,317
Is Michael okay?

320
00:17:07,401 --> 00:17:11,196
Yeah, it's Stuart. Bernie's dad gave him a hug
and cracked a rib.

321
00:17:13,115 --> 00:17:15,492
Yeah, okay. We'll meet you in the lobby.

322
00:17:19,246 --> 00:17:21,582
How long is it gonna take you to get ready?

323
00:17:21,665 --> 00:17:23,459
I just need like five or 10 minutes.

324
00:17:23,542 --> 00:17:26,462
-Really?
-No. What is wrong with you?

325
00:17:29,923 --> 00:17:36,180
[announcer] And in the field of physics,
Dr. Amy Farrah Fowler and Dr. Sheldon Cooper

326
00:17:36,263 --> 00:17:39,183
for their discovery of super-asymmetry.

327
00:17:40,726 --> 00:17:42,352
[Penny whistles]

328
00:17:42,436 --> 00:17:44,563
[Penny &amp; Howard whoop]

329
00:17:48,025 --> 00:17:50,027
[whooping]

330
00:17:51,111 --> 00:17:54,782
-Just to be clear, this isn't a date.
-Yeah. I know.

331
00:17:55,741 --> 00:17:57,326
Then why are you holding my hand?

332
00:17:59,703 --> 00:18:03,248
Thank you, Your Majesties.
Thank you to the Nobel Committee.

333
00:18:04,583 --> 00:18:06,627
We are deeply honored.

334
00:18:08,045 --> 00:18:12,841
I would just like to take this moment
to say to all the young girls out there

335
00:18:12,925 --> 00:18:16,178
who dream about science as a profession,

336
00:18:16,261 --> 00:18:17,096
go for it.

337
00:18:18,180 --> 00:18:21,809
It is the greatest job in the world.

338
00:18:23,352 --> 00:18:26,105
And if anybody tells you you can't,
don't listen.

339
00:18:29,733 --> 00:18:33,070
And now, speaking of not listening...

340
00:18:34,530 --> 00:18:36,365
my husband, Dr. Sheldon Cooper.

341
00:18:37,950 --> 00:18:38,784
Sheldon.

342
00:18:39,993 --> 00:18:43,121
Did you finish? Great job. [clears throat]

343
00:18:45,374 --> 00:18:46,917
Thank you, Dr. Fowler.

344
00:18:49,878 --> 00:18:54,758
I have a very long
and somewhat self-centered speech here...

345
00:18:56,552 --> 00:18:58,345
but I'd like to set it aside.

346
00:18:58,428 --> 00:19:00,973
-[Penny] Yeah! [whoops]
-Sheldon!

347
00:19:04,518 --> 00:19:07,604
Because this honor
doesn't just belong to me.

348
00:19:09,439 --> 00:19:14,736
I wouldn't be up here if it weren't
for some very important people in my life.

349
00:19:14,903 --> 00:19:21,827
Beginning with my mother and father,
Meemaw, brother and sister.

350
00:19:24,830 --> 00:19:26,748
And my other family,

351
00:19:26,832 --> 00:19:28,667
who I'm so happy to have here with us.

352
00:19:30,210 --> 00:19:32,171
Is that Buffy the Vampire Slayer?

353
00:19:39,261 --> 00:19:44,725
I was under a misapprehension
that my accomplishments were mine alone.

354
00:19:46,977 --> 00:19:48,896
Nothing could be further from the truth.

355
00:19:50,856 --> 00:19:55,402
I have been encouraged, sustained,

356
00:19:55,485 --> 00:19:58,864
inspired and tolerated,

357
00:19:58,947 --> 00:20:00,782
not only by my wife,

358
00:20:02,075 --> 00:20:04,786
but by the greatest group of friends
anyone ever had.

359
00:20:08,206 --> 00:20:10,000
I'd like to ask them to stand.

360
00:20:12,419 --> 00:20:13,962
Dr. Rajesh Koothrappali.

361
00:20:18,508 --> 00:20:20,719
Dr. Bernadette Rostenkowski-Wolowitz.

362
00:20:25,140 --> 00:20:27,017
Astronaut Howard Wolowitz.

363
00:20:31,897 --> 00:20:34,399
And my two dearest friends in the world...

364
00:20:36,318 --> 00:20:37,319
Penny Hofstadter...

365
00:20:43,033 --> 00:20:44,826
and Dr. Leonard Hofstadter.

366
00:20:49,414 --> 00:20:52,793
I was there
the moment Leonard and Penny met.

367
00:20:52,876 --> 00:20:56,546
He said to me that their babies
would be smart and beautiful.

368
00:20:59,007 --> 00:21:02,761
And now that they're expecting,
I have no doubt that will be the case.

369
00:21:02,844 --> 00:21:06,473
Thanks, Sheldon.
I haven't told my parents yet, but thanks.

370
00:21:07,975 --> 00:21:09,142
Oh.

371
00:21:09,226 --> 00:21:10,352
Um, sorry.

372
00:21:11,645 --> 00:21:14,606
Don't tell anyone that last thing.
That's a secret.

373
00:21:16,149 --> 00:21:18,568
Howard, Bernadette,

374
00:21:19,695 --> 00:21:23,490
Raj, Penny, Leonard,

375
00:21:23,573 --> 00:21:26,827
I apologize if I haven't been the friend
you deserve.

376
00:21:28,912 --> 00:21:32,708
But I want you to know, in my way...

377
00:21:35,043 --> 00:21:35,961
I love you all.

378
00:21:39,506 --> 00:21:40,590
And I love you.

379
00:21:44,803 --> 00:21:45,679
Thank you.

380
00:21:46,722 --> 00:21:47,681
[crowd applauding]

381
00:21:54,021 --> 00:21:55,272
[chattering]

382
00:21:55,355 --> 00:21:59,860
♪ Our whole universe was in
A hot, dense state ♪

383
00:21:59,943 --> 00:22:06,366
♪ Then nearly 14 billion years ago
Expansion started-- Wait ♪

384
00:22:06,450 --> 00:22:10,829
♪ The Earth began to cool
The autotrophs began to drool ♪

385
00:22:10,912 --> 00:22:14,499
♪ Neanderthals developed tools
We built a wall ♪

386
00:22:14,583 --> 00:22:17,502
♪ We built the pyramids ♪

387
00:22:17,586 --> 00:22:22,841
♪ Math, science, history
Unraveling the mystery ♪

388
00:22:22,924 --> 00:22:27,054
♪ That all started with a big bang ♪

